
3623 Problem :-
You are given a 2D integer array points, where points[i] = [xi, yi] represents the coordinates of the ith point on the Cartesian plane.
A horizontal trapezoid is a convex quadrilateral with at least one pair of horizontal sides (i.e. parallel to the x-axis). Two lines are parallel if and only if they have the same slope.
Return the number of unique horizontal trapezoids that can be formed by choosing any four distinct points from points.
Since the answer may be very large, return it modulo 109 + 7.

Example 1:-
Input: points = [[1,0],[2,0],[3,0],[2,2],[3,2]]
Output: 3
Explanation:-
There are three distinct ways to pick four points that form a horizontal trapezoid:
Using points [1,0], [2,0], [3,2], and [2,2].
Using points [2,0], [3,0], [3,2], and [2,2].
Using points [1,0], [3,0], [3,2], and [2,2].

Example 2:-
Input: points = [[0,0],[1,0],[0,1],[2,1]]
Output: 1

Explanation:-
There is only one horizontal trapezoid that can be formed.

Constraints:-
4 <= points.length <= 105
–108 <= xi, yi <= 108
All points are pairwise distinct.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Solution :-

Intuition
A trapezoid (with parallel horizontal bases) is determined by two distinct x-coordinates x1 and x2, and two distinct rows (y-values) that both contain points at x1 and x2. So for every unordered pair (x1,x2) count how many rows contain that pair; if that count is k then that pair contributes C(k,2) trapezoids. We can accumulate trapezoids incrementally: whenever we see a pair (x1,x2) again, it contributes the current count of that pair to the answer.

Approach:-
Group points by their y coordinate (map y -> list of x).
For each row (list of x), iterate over all unordered pairs (x1, x2) in that row.
Maintain a map pairCount from a packed (x1,x2) key to how many rows have seen that pair so far.
When processing a pair with current count c, add c to ans (because this new row pairs with each of those c previous rows to form c new trapezoids), then increment the stored count to c+1.
Do all additions modulo 1e9+7.

Complexity:-
Time complexity: Let the rows have sizes s1, s2, .... Time is O(sum_i si^2) because we enumerate all pairs within each row.
In worst-case (all points in one row) it’s O(n^2); typically points are spread, so this is fine for the constraints of the problem.
Space complexity: O(P) where P is the number of distinct (x1,x2) pairs encountered (worst-case O(n^2)).

Code:-

class Solution {
    public int countTrapezoids(int[][] points) {
        long mod=1000000007L;
        Map<Integer,Long> mapy=new HashMap<>();
        for(int []row:points){
            mapy.put(row[1],mapy.getOrDefault(row[1],0L)+1);
        }
        List<Long> height=new ArrayList<>();
        for(long val:mapy.values()){
            if(val>=2){
                long ways=(val*(val-1))/2;
                height.add(ways%mod);
            }
        }
        long ans=0,sum=0;
        for(long val:height){
            ans=(ans+(val*sum)%mod)%mod;
            sum=(sum+val)%mod;
        }
        return (int)ans;
    }
}
